#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""skarbnik-vendor: the vendor's command line tool for private bounties on the Skarbnik contract (NEAR testnet).

Each bounty has a mode (bounty.mode): "crash" (default; the original crashes on the secret input), "output" (the
original returns a wrong answer on it, which also covers memory safety bugs that corrupt memory without crashing) or
"rule" (a program with the call interface breaks a public rule module on a secret call sequence: injection, path
traversal, missing authorization, IDOR). In output mode the secret file holds one line "<input as hex> <expected value>
[sweep=<p>,<p>]"; in rule mode one line "<call sequence as hex> [sweep=<p>,<p>]", and bounty.rule names the public rule
module (default <module>/rule.wasm). In both, the reference fix travels with every proof (the checker compares fixes
with it), and bounty.sweep names the same private byte positions as the secret line (at most 4; each is tried with all
256 values). Each bounty has a level (bounty.level, default 2): 2 = the contract checks a Groth16 proof itself; 1 = the contract
trusts OutLayer running the pinned verifier program on the succinct proof (no Groth16 step, so no Docker and no 7.6 GiB
here); 0 = two registered verifiers decide (today both are run by the project).

A vendor should not have to be a cryptography operator. Commands:
  init     ask a few questions (or copy an older config with --from) and write a bounty config (TOML), outside the repo
  check    the preflight: module, public tests, the original is buggy on the secret, the reference fix passes on several
           seeds, memory for the prover mode, the checker id on the allowlist, the vendor's balance, and the verdict
           window against K times the measured proof time; prints a preview and refuses with a plain reason.
           With --candidate F it only runs the native checker on that one file and prints its verdict (no chain)
  prove0   Proof 0 of the reference fix: prove, shrink to succinct, then (Level 2 only) Groth16, verify, the contract
           arguments (mode gpu: prove and shrink on a rented GPU pod, Groth16 here); one job at a
           time, memory watched (scripts/s2_measure.sh)
  publish  shows the preview, asks for confirmation, creates and opens the bounty (Level 2: open_with_groth16; Level 1:
           open_with_outlayer, attaching the contract's min_deposit; Level 0: opens when two verifiers attest), prints the
           transactions
  watch    the auto prover: polls the chain for revealed attempts on the bounties in the watch config, proves each one
           (oldest first) with the attempt's seed and submission id, checks the verdict and the secret commitment, and
           settles it by the bounty's level: Level 2 submits the Groth16 proof (submit_groth16), Level 1 sends the
           succinct proof to OutLayer (check_with_outlayer, attaching min_deposit, held and returned), Level 0 posts the
           verdict for the verifiers (post_verdict); warns when a window is at risk. Prover
           mode cpu proves on this machine; mode gpu proves and shrinks on a rented GPU pod per attempt
           (tools/skarbnik_gpu.py: secret in the pod's RAM only, pod always terminated, 45 minute cap, spend cap),
           then wraps to Groth16 here (Level 2). When no GPU is free it waits 5 minutes, then 10 between tries, while
           at least 2 hours of the verdict window are left; any other failure gets two rounds of two pods. At the end
           it writes a loud ALERT line, kept in the state for `status`, and never gives up silently. With
           gpu.cpu_fallback = true it proves on this machine's CPU instead of waiting or stopping
  status   each watched bounty and attempt, its state and the next action needed

Secrets (the secret input, the salt, the reference fix) stay on the machine that proves, in files of mode 600 outside
the repository, and are never printed. The salt is made and read by `skarbnik-host --bounty <name>` in
~/.kintsugi-salts/. Transactions are signed by NEAR CLI (near-cli-rs) from its legacy keychain. Python 3.11+, no
packages. Usage: tools/skarbnik-vendor <command> --config <file.toml> (see tools/vendor.example.toml, docs/VENDOR.md).
"""
